# Project Overview

This project analyzes critic scores, user scores, 
and sales data for the top 400 video games released since 1977.
The goal is to explore whether there was a “golden age” of video games by identifying 
the years when games received the highest ratings from critics and users. 
The project also examines video game sales to explore trends in the gaming industry and 
the relationship between critical reception and commercial success.

# Key Questions
Which years produced the highest-rated video games?
Do critic and user scores agree on the best years for gaming?
How have video game sales changed over time?
Is there a relationship between game ratings and commercial success?

# Key Finding

1. Best-Selling Games
  > 'Wii Sports', released by Nintendo for the Wii in 2006, was the best-selling game in the dataset,
    with 82.9 million units sold—more than twice the sales of most other top-selling games.
  > 'Super Mario Bros.', released for the NES in 1985, ranked second with 40.24 million units sold.
     Despite being released more than four decades ago, 
     Super Mario Bros. remains one of the best-selling games in the dataset. 
     This highlights the long-term commercial success and enduring popularity of Nintendo's classic titles.

2. Top Years by Critic Score
  > 1998 stood out as the highest-rated year among years with multiple game releases. 
    The year had 19 games with an average critic score of 9.32.
    In comparison, 2011 had the highest number of game releases in this analysis, with 26 games, but its average critic score was 8.76, ranking sixth.
    This suggests that a larger number of releases did not necessarily result in higher average critical ratings.

3. Identifying a Golden Year
  > To identify years with consistently strong game releases, 
    I limited the analysis to years with at least four games released and compared average critic and user scores.
    1998 emerged as the strongest candidate for a “golden year.” 
    It had the smallest difference between average critic and user scores, at just 0.08, 
    suggesting that professional critics and players were highly aligned in their evaluation of games released that year.

    An interesting contrast appeared in 1997. 
    Although only eight games were included, the year had the lowest average critic score (7.93) among the selected years 
    while achieving a relatively high average user score (9.5). This represents a substantial gap between professional and user evaluations.

    **This raises an interesting question:**

    >> What factors cause professional critics and players to evaluate the same games so differently?
